2. Test cases description
The geometric features of the MTP test article are: channel base b 1⁄4 1 mm, channel height h 1⁄4 3 mm, rib base tw 1⁄4 1 mm, internal wall thickness sw 1⁄4 1 mm and channel length L 1⁄4 300 mm, which corresponds to 200 hydraulic diameters. The imposed inlet conditions are the static temperature Ti and the mass flow rate m_ , while the outflow condition is the static pressure pe. The inner wall boundary condition is that of imposed heat flux qw while adiabatic conditions are considered at the outer-wall interface for both the wall and the coolant and at the lateral walls. In particular, the imposed entering heat flux at the inner-wall is 11.1 MW/m2. The schematic of the MTP test article with the enforced boundary conditions for the coolant and solid domain is shown in Fig. 2. The coolant flow is supercritical methane while the solid material properties refer to a high-conductivity copper alloy with a thermal conductivity of kw 1⁄4 387.6 W/m K, which is considered constant with temperature. Finally, wall surface is considered either smooth or with a certain level of equivalent-sand-grain roughness. To describe the different MTP operative conditions, different test cases have been considered, as described in Table 1. All the considered test cases share the same inlet temperature Ti 1⁄4 120 K and the same mass flow rate m_ 1⁄4 20 g=s; while two different levels of exit pressure are considered, pe 1⁄4 100 bar and pe 1⁄4 53.4 bar, in order to describe the behavior of methane “sufficiently” far from the critical pressure, and in near-critical condition. Finally, different values of wall roughness, 0, 4.5, and 10 mm, are considered.
3. Modeling
A Conjugate Heat Transfer (CHT) model based on the coupled numerical integration of the NaviereStokes equations for the coolant flow and the Fourier's equation for the heat conduction in the solid material is adopted in the present study [16,17]. In particular, the coolant-flow solver integrates the Reynolds Averaged NaviereStokes equations, written in the conservation form, by a Godunov-type finite volume scheme, which is second-order accurate in space [18,19]. Its main feature is the capability to deal with any compressible fluid whose behavior is represented by a generic equation of state, as the fluid thermodynamic properties are stored in a look-up table. Turbulence is computed according to the oneequation model of SpalarteAllmaras for both smooth and rough
surfaces [20,21]. Validation of the coolant-flow solver has been reported by comparison made with available experimental data on supercritical nitrogen [18,22] and transcritical hydrogen [10]. The coupled solution of the coolant flow and the wall heat conduction is achieved by an iterative procedure in which the wall temperature distribution obtained by a finite-volume solver, which is secondorder accurate in space, of the Fourier's thermal problem is used as boundary condition for the coolant flow computations [16]. Finally, the thermodynamic behavior of methane, which is the considered fluid in the present study, is described by the ModifiedeBenedicteWebbeRubin equation of state and proper relations for the fluid viscosity and thermal conductivity, which are taken from Ref. [23].
4. Grid sensitivity
Numerical error assessment of the CHT simulations has been investigated by a grid sensitivity analysis performed by means of three levels of grid refinement. The most refined grid of the coolant domain, referred to as fine grid, is composed of 64 256 64 volumes along the half-base, the height and the axial direction of the channel. Taking advantage from the flow symmetry with respect to the vertical mid-plane (Fig. 2), only one-half of the physical domain is discretized. The volumes are clustered near the walls to accurately describe the turbulent boundary layers while the axial distribution is uniform. The computational fine grid of the solid domain is composed of 4992 64 volumes along the cross section and the axial direction of the channel; moreover, in each coordinate direction, the mesh distribution is uniform. Also in this case only one-half of the physical domain is discretized (Fig. 2). The medium grid is obtained by removing one node out of two, in each coordinate direction and for both the coolant and the solid domain, from the fine grid. In the same way a coarse grid is obtained from the medium grid. Computational mesh of the channel cross section is presented in Fig. 3 for the medium and fine grids. In particular, the grid sensitivity analysis has been performed on test cases 1 and 2 (Table 1) because of the relatively pronounced dependence of their solutions, especially the wall temperature, on the mesh refinements. The wall temperature distribution along the channel length and at the inner side of the cooling channel is shown in Fig. 4 for the three selected meshes. More specifically, the mentioned wall temperature is taken at the symmetry-plane, being negligible the temperature variations along the inner wall. Looking at Fig. 4, it is evident that the mesh refinement has a relevant impact on the wall temperature evaluation: for the test case with ε 1⁄4 0 mm, wall temperature presents a peak value which increases by 95 K passing from the rough to the medium grid or by 24 K passing from the medium to the fine grid. For the test case with ε 1⁄4 4.5 mm, these quantities reduces to 68 K and 14 K. Moreover, in both cases the position of the maximum value shifts upstream. The achieved asymptotic convergence behavior suggests that a further grid refinement would increase the peak values by ~6 K in case of ε 1⁄4 0 mm and by ~3 K in case of ε 1⁄4 4.5 mm. The relatively small improvement of the wall temperature prediction that could be achieved with a further grid refinement, allows one to adopt the fine mesh to describe such test cases.
