The remaining test cases, which do not exhibit a peak value of the wall temperature, and for which only two grid levels have been considered, present a much milder dependence of the wall temperature on the grid refinement. In particular, the maximum wall temperature difference between coarse and medium grid is 8 K for test case 3, 30 K for test case 4, and 9 K for test case 5. The relatively small improvement of the wall temperature prediction that is expected with the fine grid, allows one to adopt the medium mesh to describe such test cases. As for the other variables relevant to the analyzed problem (such as, coolant pressure drop and temperature gain), their numerical estimation presents a rather weak dependence on the mesh refinement. For instance, passing from the coarse to the medium grid, pressure drop variation is below 4% for the test cases
with smooth wall (TC1 and TC4), below 1.5% for the test cases with ε 1⁄4 4.5 mm (TC2 and TC5), and below 0.1% for the test cases with ε 1⁄4 10 mm (TC3). The reason for a less demanding mesh refinement with increasing wall roughness is that the wall roughness diminishes the gradients at wall and thus less numerical resolution is required. This can be seen also in Fig. 4, where wall temperature dependence on mesh refinement is more evident in case of ε 1⁄4 0 mm rather than in case ε 1⁄4 4.5 mm.
5. Results
5.1. Reference test case (TC1)
The thermal and fluid-dynamic behavior of TC1 is shown by temperature fields of both coolant and solid material in Fig. 5, and coolant specific heat at constant pressure fields in Fig. 6. In these figures, the evolution of the variables is shown at different crosssections, from the inlet toward the exit, which are located every 6 cm along the streamwise direction. Methane enters the channel in a highly-compressed state; in fact, for the considered test case the resulting supercritical inlet pressure is pi 1⁄4 58.1 bar and, due to the low-temperature inlet condition, Ti 1⁄4 120 K, its density is nearly
415 kg/m3. Then, as it is heated-up by the entering heat flux from the hot walls located in the lower part of the channel section, the fluid accelerates while its pressure decreases due to the friction force acting on the channel wall and the entering heat flux. The pressure distribution (not shown here) is one-dimensional like, that is, it is only a function of abscissa x, regardless of the threedimensional channel geometry and the asymmetric thermal boundary conditions. The one-dimensional behavior of pressure is a direct consequence of the constant cross-section, straight channel geometry. Fig. 5 shows that the high temperature flow region grows as the fluid moves from the inlet to the exit section, but it remains close to the hot wall along the whole length. As a consequence, the fluid far from the bottom wall is nearly at the inlet condition and an evident non-uniform thermal distribution occurs. Fig. 5 also permits to analyze the wall temperature distribution in the solid material. In every channel section, wall temperature exhibits a maximum value which is located on the inner wall and on the coolant symmetry-plane, that is, at the maximum distance from the solid ribs. This is because the thermal resistance of the coolant flow is larger than that of the solid ribs. For this reason the heat transfer rate, which entirely enters the coolant flow through its perimeter, flows toward the solid ribs more than toward the
