value, ranging from the minimum value of 120 K to the maximum value of x800 K, thermodynamic parameters variation across a channel section is similar to the isobaric properties variation. Therefore, for a given cross section, constant pressure specific heat shows a maximum located at the pseudo-critical temperature. The presence of a maximum value of cp is clearly seen in Fig. 6. Moreover, because the specific heat peak is more pronounced as the pressure approaches the critical value, the maximum value of cp is present at the channel exit where the pressure reaches its minimum value (pe 1⁄4 53.4 bar). At this pressure level the maximum value of specific heat is ten times higher than the perfect gas value (that is, the value at T [ Tc). Note that the relatively wide variation of the specific heat and the presence of a peak value, which virtually separates the “gas-like” region close to the walls to the “liquid-like” region, are the typical behavior of the transcritical-fluid flow. More
