specifically, the heat transfer deterioration is generated by the gaseous film close to the wall which is characterized, because of its low density, by low thermal capacity. The gaseous film “insulates” the liquid in the core of the tube, causing a very high wall temperature and poor wall heat transfer. Downstream, the further fluid heating enlarges the low density region and, as a consequence, the bulk density decreases and the average fluid velocity increases. The increase of fluid velocity improves the heat transfer and, in turn, the wall temperature decreases. In case of TC1 the maximum wall temperature occurs at section x 1⁄4 16.4 cm.
5.2. Coolant pressure effect
The comparison of the results relevant to test cases 1 and 4 permits to appreciate the role of the fluid pressure level in the case of smooth wall. The comparison is of particular interest because of the relatively vicinity of the pressure levels to the critical pressure. In the case of TC1 the ratio of the exit pressure to the critical value is 1.16 and in the case TC4 it is 2.17. This suggests that the first test case will be much more affected by the large properties variation that occurs in the near-critical region than TC4. This is demonstrated in Fig. 7(a) where the inner-wall temperature distribution on the symmetry plane and along the channel length is presented. In case of TC1 the wall temperature presents a peak value of 842 K at the axial abscissa 16.4 cm while in the other case no peak value is present; in this case the wall temperature progressively increases along the axial abscissa and its maximum, located at the exit section, is 567 K. To better visualize the difference between the two considered test cases, the coolant bulk temperature and specific heat at constant pressure are presented in Fig. 7(b). The bulk temperature is defined as the mass-flux weighted average of the fluid temperature, over the tube cross section. In this figure, also the pseudo-critical temperature relevant to the two test cases is drawn. Note that the two values of the pseudo-critical temperature differ because of the different pressure level of the two test cases: it is nearly 196 K for TC1 and 218 K for TC4. Fluid bulk temperature exceeds this value at section x 1⁄4 22.5 cm for TC1 and x 1⁄4 25.5 cm for TC4. Here it is reminded that the fluid bulk temperature excess of the pseudo-critical value is the prerequisite for the heat transfer deterioration (Section 1). However, Fig. 7(a) clearly shows that deterioration takes place only in TC1. The different behavior between TC1 and TC4 must be sought in the pressure level of the two test cases. In case of TC1 the exit pressure is rather close to the
critical value (“only” 16% larger than the critical value) and thus the fluid properties variation is so intense to induce heat transfer deterioration; it is not the case of TC4. In fact, looking at Fig. 7(b), it is evident that the streamwise variation of the fluid properties is dramatic only for TC1: the specific heat reaches a value more than 7 times higher than at entrance in case of TC1 and less than 2 times in case of TC4. Looking at this figure, it also important to notice that in case of TC1 the position of the specific heat peak (x 1⁄4 22.5 cm) is more downstream than the position of the maximum wall temperature (x 1⁄4 16.4 cm). This is because the performed analysis is based on fluid bulk variables, that are mass-flux weighted averages across the channel cross section; in reality, difference of the fluid properties is present also in the transversal directions, especially in the case of near critical fluids. This can be appreciated in Fig. 6, where it is shown that a maximum cp contour line is present in every cross section the specific heat presents a peak value region. Finally, for what concerns the coolant pressure drop Dp0, it is 2.8 bar in case of TC1 and 2.0 bar in the case of TC4. Thus, it is possible to say that, for a given coolant mass flow rate and entering heat transfer, the coolant pressure drop increases as the coolant pressure level approaches the critical value: pressure drop reduces by almost 30% passing from pe/pc 1⁄4 1.16 (TC1) to pe/pc 1⁄4 2.17 (TC4).
5.3. Roughness effect
The comparison between the results of TC1, TC2, and TC3 permits to appreciate, for a given pressure level and entering heat transfer, the role of the surface roughness level on the cooling performances. Surface is smooth in case of TC1 and is rough in cases TC2 (ε 1⁄4 4.5 mm) and TC3 (ε 1⁄4 10 mm). In Fig. 8(a) the inner-wall temperature distribution on the symmetry plane and along the channel length is presented for the three considered test cases. Test case with smooth wall (TC1) presents the peculiar behavior of the heat transfer deterioration which has been discussed in detail in the previous sections. Deterioration can also be recognized in TC2 because the wall temperature presents a peak value of 639 K at the axial abscissa 22.0 cm, but not in TC3 because the wall temperature progressively increases along the axial abscissa and its maximum, located at the exit section, is 396 K. The surface roughness effect on the wall temperature is easily found: thanks to the increased turbulence production with surface roughness, the wall temperature decreases. Moreover, there exists a threshold value of the roughness beyond which the heat transfer deterioration vanishes. For the
