where T is the fluid bulk temperature, and Tw and qw are the average wall temperature and heat flux of a channel section. The hc behavior in the very first part of the channel is driven by the mechanical boundary layer growth: initially it decreases and then it increases after reaching a minimum value. This is clearly visible in case of TC2 and TC3 (Fig. 8(a)), where the mentioned minimum value is reached at sections x 1⁄4 2.3 cm and x 1⁄4 2.6 cm. In case of TC1, the heat transfer coefficient minimum, located at 16.4 cm, is not related to the boundary layer growth but to the heat transfer deterioration. In fact, the minimum value of hc corresponds to the maximum value of the wall temperature (Fig. 8(a)) and is equal to 7.8 kW/m2 K, that is, almost three times lower than the inlet value and two times lower than the exit value. In the other two test cases, namely TC2 and TC3, the heat transfer coefficient presents a substantially different behavior with respect to that of TC1. In particular, after the boundary layer growth, the heat transfer coefficient increases up to a maximum and than decreases to a minimum value. More specifically, in case of TC2 the maximum value is equal to 50.5 kW/m2 K, located at x 1⁄4 7.3 cm, and the minimum is 12.5 kW/m2 K, located at x 1⁄4 22.0 cm. Note that this minimum hc value, being larger than that of TC1, causes a maximum wall temperature which is about 200 K less than the maximum wall temperature of TC1. This is because of the beneficial effect of the surface roughness on the wall temperature distribution (Fig. 8(a)). For the same reason, TC3 presents an even larger heat transfer coefficient characterized by a plateau of ~97 kW/m2 K, between sections at x x 8 cm and x x 14 cm, followed by a monotonic decrement up to the exit value of ~39 kW/m2 K. The larger large value of hc in case of TC3, induced by the relatively high value of surface roughness, prevents from heat transfer deterioration (Fig. 8(a)). Of course, the wall temperature reduction, up to the suppression of the heat transfer deterioration, which can be realized thanks to the increment of the surface roughness level, is paid in terms of pressure drop Dp0. In fact, while it is 2.8 bar in case of ε 1⁄4 0 mm
(TC1), it raises up to 3.5 bar (25%) in case of ε 1⁄4 4.5 mm (TC2) and up to 5.2 bar (86%) in case of ε 1⁄4 10 mm (TC3). Roughness effect has been investigated also in case of higher pressure, that is, pe 1⁄4 100 bar. In particular, surface is smooth in case of TC4 and has a roughness level of ε 1⁄4 4.5 mm in the case TC5. As already seen in Section 5.2, the wall temperature distribution of TC4 (Fig. 7(a)) is not affected by the heat transfer deterioration and the maximum wall temperature is 567 K (at the exit section). The surface roughness level of ε 1⁄4 4.5 mm is able to reduce the wall temperature of ~140 K all along the channel length; the resulting maximum value, always at the exit, is 431 K. As for the pressure drop, it is 2.0 bar in case of ε 1⁄4 0 mm (TC4) and it raises up to 3.2 bar (60%) in case of ε 1⁄4 4.5 mm (TC5). As already mentioned in Section 5.2, for a given coolant mass flow rate and entering heat transfer, the coolant pressure drop increases as the coolant pressure level approaches the critical value. Here it is also demonstrated that the roughness effect on the pressure drop is more marked in case of larger coolant pressure level: in fact, passing from ε 1⁄4 0 mm to ε 1⁄4 4.5 mm, Dp0 increases by 60% in case of pe 1⁄4 100 bar and by 25% in case of pe 1⁄4 53.4 bar.
6. Conclusion
The present study has been focused on the near-critical operative condition of methane inside a rectangular cooling channel, which can be representative of a rocket engine regenerative system. For the given nominal heat input, mass flow rate and inlet subcritical temperature, deterioration has been encountered in case of smooth surfaces and exit-to-critical pressure ratio equal to 1.16. In this case the maximum wall temperature is nearly 850 K. Different coolant pressure and surface roughness levels have been considered in order to understand their influence on the heat transfer capability of the cooling system. Numerical results have highlighted that the heat transfer deterioration is mitigated either in case of higher coolant pressure or higher surface roughness. In the latter case, an important penalty in terms of coolant pressure drop occurs. On the other hand, the increase to an exit-to-critical pressure ratio equal to 2.17 leads to a maximum wall temperature of almost 570 K and to a 30% lower pressure drop. Note that heat transfer deterioration does not occur at a surface roughness level of ε 1⁄4 10 mm, which however yields to an increase of almost 90% of the coolant pressure drop. At lower roughness level, deterioration does not disappears but its effect on the maximum wall temperature is significantly reduced.
